## 3. Uniform compact-age source lemma

Set s=t-epsilon^2 tau with 0<=tau<=A, and denote x_i by x_i,epsilon(t,tau).
Let E_r be the exact no-event N=6 Riesz blocks and Q_r the Hermitian energy
blocks. The original canonical birth expansion, now as an operator on a
weighted low trajectory, gives

    E_0 L_i v4(s)=sqrt(kappa) J6,0 [B_i,S a4(s)+o(1)],
    E_1 L_i v4(s)=sqrt(kappa) epsilon [R_i,S a4(s)+o(1)],
    E_2 L_i v4(s)=O(epsilon^2).                            (8)

The o(1) are uniform on this compact tau interval. To check that using the
no-event N=4 input changes no leading coefficient: (2) differs from U4 a4
by O(epsilon^3) there. Multiplying by bounded j_i and epsilon^-1 makes an
O(epsilon^2) source error. The canonical high-one source has size epsilon,
so that error is smaller. E_r-Q_r=O(epsilon^3) introduces no larger term.
The high-two source bound O(epsilon^2) needs no stronger actual-birth cubic
cancellation. Uniform weighted bounds control the low source; every map in
(8) is a finite-hop coefficient plus a uniformly analytic remainder.

On age epsilon^2 tau, the first-high propagator is, in exact coordinates,

    exp(-i delta tau/epsilon^2) exp(tau[Z_S+O(epsilon^2)]).

The uniformly bounded Z_S converge strongly to Z; the exponential converges
strongly uniformly on compact tau intervals. The rapidly oscillating scalar
phase is retained, but cancels from its norm. The other no-event components
are bounded by exact contraction and uniformly bounded projectors. The
operator estimates ||H E_0||=O(epsilon^-2), ||H E_1,2||=O(epsilon^-4) imply
that the low and high-two terms vanish after multiplication of the energy
vector by epsilon^3. The exact Hermitian first-high center is
delta epsilon^-4 with relative O(epsilon^2) correction. Thus

    ||x_i,epsilon(t,tau)||^2 -> kappa b_i S4(t),
    epsilon^6 ||H x_i,epsilon(t,tau)||^2
          -> kappa delta^2 ||exp(tau Z) R_i u4(t)||^2.      (9)

Both limits are uniform for 0<=tau<=A. For the norm assertion the low
physical-time propagation over epsilon^2 tau tends strongly to the identity
on the compact weighted source path; its diagonal KD term is controlled on
that domain. Merely bounding its growing operator norm would not prove this.

For the raw mean, Hermitian band orthogonality, low-block norm O(epsilon^-2),
and high-one probability O(epsilon^2) give

    |<x_i,H x_i>|=O(epsilon^-2).                           (10)

The limit norm in (9) is strictly positive. Therefore the subnormalized
within-path variance

    V_H(x)=||H x||^2-|<x,Hx>|^2/||x||^2

satisfies the same epsilon^6 limit as (9). No Fisher/variance equivalence for
a mixed ensemble, no trace-norm continuity of unbounded moments, and no
fixed-S numerical extrapolation are used.

## 4. Full-ensemble pointwise variance lower bound

The variance of a normalized mixture is the mixture of its within-component
variances plus the nonnegative variance of its component means. This law
applies to the exact continuous decomposition (1), with the N=4 and N=8
components and all earlier first births. Hence

    Var_H(rho_epsilon(t))
       >=sum_i integral_(t-A epsilon^2)^t V_H(x_i(s,t)) ds.

Using ds=epsilon^2 d tau and (9)-(10) proves, for every fixed A>0,

    liminf epsilon^4 Var_H(rho_epsilon(t))
      >= kappa delta^2 I_A(t),
    I_A(t)=sum_i integral_0^A ||exp(tau Z) R_i u4(t)||^2 d tau. (11)

This is a positive, fixed-positive-time lower bound in the N=4-start full
original ensemble. It does not resolve the pointwise variance asymptotic of
the different problem initialized in a normalized N=6 actual-birth state.

Since Z is bounded, exp(tau Z) has inverse of norm at most exp(M tau).
Equations (6)-(7) therefore give the explicit bound

    I_A(t)>=72 S4(t) [1-exp(-2 M A)]/(2 M).                 (12)

Taking the supremum of the valid fixed-A inequalities, with epsilon tending
to zero first for each A, yields in particular

    liminf epsilon^4 Var_H(rho_epsilon(t))
       >=36 kappa delta^2 exp(-48 kappa t)/(288 delta+kappa)>0. (13)

The coefficient is deliberately crude and not optimal. More generally the
right side of (11) can be replaced by kappa delta^2 I_infinity(t), interpreted
as the monotone supremum, possibly infinite until integrability is proved.
No compact-time limit is evaluated at growing tau; no finite-spin uniform
high-sector gap or tail estimate is assumed. No matching upper bound or full
asymptotic coefficient is proved here.

## 5. Energy carried by a small recent-birth subensemble

Define rho6,recent^A(t) by restricting the exact integral (1) to these ages.
Let Q1 be the physical Hermitian N=6 first-high projection. Its compact-age
source limit also gives

    epsilon^-4 Tr(Q1 rho6,recent^A) -> kappa I_A(t),
    Tr(H Q1 rho6,recent^A) -> kappa delta I_A(t),
    epsilon^4 Tr(H^2 Q1 rho6,recent^A) -> kappa delta^2 I_A(t). (14)

The recent-birth subensemble itself has probability O(epsilon^2), while its
first-high probability is O(epsilon^4). The latter lies at energy of order
epsilon^-4 and hence carries finite nonzero ordinary mean energy and a
second moment of order epsilon^-4. Formula (14) is an energy accounting
statement about this specified component. Because the full first-high block
has positive energy for small epsilon, it also supplies a lower bound on
that full block's energy expectation. It does not by itself subtract the
common low rotor energy, establish the limit of the full ordinary mean, or
give an apparatus ground-relative energy cost.

The earlier normalized-birth ordinary mean result is compatible with (14):
each fixed positive age excludes the recent ages of order epsilon^2. One
cannot exchange that pointwise age limit with the full birth-time integral
without controlling this contribution.

## 6. Physical and evidence boundaries

All Hamiltonians, spaces, compensation, scaling, time structure, canonical
preparation and the complete formation instrument remain supplied. The
result accounts for microscopic system energy, not an interacting total
Hamiltonian or a selected reservoir. A large mixed-state variance does not
establish energetic Fisher information: averaging over unobserved birth
times can dephase energy bands. No additional apparatus coherence bound is
inferred from (13). The separate N=4 no-first-birth witness has its own
conditional Fisher bound and different divergence power.

The proof must receive a selective independent reconstruction of the source
expansion, the full fast generator, compact-age limits, law of total variance,
all-mark sum and the order of limits before publication. The new N=4 and
preparation-uniform parents remain explicitly provisional until their reviews
complete. Any finite toy control tests only the specified source-convolution
mechanism; it cannot certify the cube's joint limit or physical selection.
